meta {
  name: Create a book
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

post {
  url: {{host}}/books
  body: json
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "title": "Refactoring",
    "isbn": "{{seedIsbn}}",
    "author": "Martin Fowler",
    "totalCopies": 1
  }
}

script:pre-request {
  // A fresh, checksum-valid ISBN-13 per run, so this request always creates a book and always
  // stores bookId. A fixed one would answer 409 on the second run - correctly, since the ISBN is
  // unique - and then every request below would run against a bookId that was never filled in.
  //
  // The checksum matters: the API verifies it rather than only the length, so a random 13 digits
  // would be refused as an invalid ISBN.
  const digits = String(Date.now()).slice(-12).padStart(12, "0");
  const sum = [...digits].reduce((total, d, i) => total + Number(d) * (i % 2 === 0 ? 1 : 3), 0);

  bru.setVar("seedIsbn", digits + ((10 - (sum % 10)) % 10));
}

script:post-response {
  if (res.body.data && res.body.data.id) {
    bru.setVar("bookId", res.body.data.id);
  }
}

docs {
  201. Stores the new book's id as bookId, which every other request in this collection uses.

  totalCopies is 1 on purpose: it is what makes a second loan attempt demonstrate the
  availability refusal rather than quietly succeeding twice.

  The ISBN is validated by checksum, not only by length, and stored normalised - so
  978-0-13-475759-9 and 9780134757599 are the same book and the second one is a 409. To seed a
  specific title, replace {{seedIsbn}} in the body with the ISBN you want.
}
